35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings remind us that expertise is a fragile thing. While we have plenty of those who can code, the seasoned hands for phenotyping have vanished into industry. We must be mindful of this void.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I've noticed a similar imbalance in costs. While some areas, like computing and glasshouse space, are still well-subsidized, others—especially animal-unit rates and shipping—have become much more expensive for the researcher to cover.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"I found some worrying stuff! The spectroscopy suite was down for eleven weeks last year because of a contract issue, and the glasshouses are totally full. Some tools are just not ready for new projects!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems the universe has a penchant for chaos. My findings suggest that biological dependencies—specifically live colonies and field sites—are far more prone to catastrophic delays than computational risks. Nature, it seems, does not follow a project timeline.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"My findings suggest that ""catalogue-style"" projects—those creating lasting resources—have far more enduring utility than those focused on specific mechanisms, which often end without resolving their central questions. We should prioritize projects with long-term, tangible deliverables.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Midori, @Kenji, your observations on costs and equipment are vital. It seems we face a paradox: we have the space and the code, but perhaps not the hands or the tools to bridge them.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Servas, exactly. It means a project relying heavily on animal work or shipping might struggle more than one focused on simulations or botany, even with the same grant amount. We should be careful about that.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, that's a great point! If the glasshouses are already full and the equipment is breaking down, a project that needs those things might be in big trouble, no matter how cool the idea is!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, your enthusiasm for the ""cool idea"" is charming, but we must be the adults in the room. A brilliant theory is merely a daydream if the researcher is spent fighting floods or waiting for a technician.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, I agree. A brilliant theory is a liability if it cannot be realized. We must favor the project that provides a concrete resource over one that promises a breakthrough but risks ending in ambiguity.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,It is a gentle reminder that the most beautiful blueprint is useless without the craftsmen to build it. We must seek a project that aligns its ambitions with the actual strength of our current workforce.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Midori,,I agree. If we can find a proposal that leverages the subsidized areas—like computing or botany—it will be much more sustainable and less stressful for the researcher to manage.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I totally agree! Let's look for a project that doesn't need the broken stuff or the full glasshouses. If they can use the things that actually work, they'll be way happier and faster!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. We should prioritize a proposal that exhibits a certain logistical humility—one that avoids the volatility of field sites and live colonies in favor of more stable, controlled environments. Stability is the silent partner of success.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"Stability is indeed preferable, but utility is paramount. We should look for a proposal that not only avoids these logistical traps but also promises a tangible, lasting contribution to the field, rather than a speculative result.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Servas,,"Let us hold these truths in our hearts as we move forward. We seek a harmony between ambition and reality, ensuring the chosen path is one that the available hands can truly walk.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Midori,,I agree. Let's keep these practical constraints in mind. I hope the candidates include a project that balances a strong scientific goal with a budget that is actually realistic given the current subsidies.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm ready! I'll be looking for the project that fits perfectly into the facilities that are actually working. Let's hope one of the candidates is smart about the tools they're using!,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I share your hope, Kenji. I shall be looking for the candidate who has wisely avoided the whims of nature and the fragility of live colonies. Let us see who among them possesses such foresight.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I will be looking for the candidate whose goals are grounded in reality. A project that delivers a lasting resource is far more valuable than a gamble on a mechanism that may never be solved.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
