60,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Looking at our options, Candidate A seems most harmonious. It creates a lasting resource and relies on sequencing and metadata, which aligns with our current abundance of coding expertise and computational strength.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Midori,,"I agree with Servas. Candidate A's focus on sequencing and metadata leverages the subsidized computing agreement, making it financially sustainable. Candidate B, while interesting, might struggle since the glasshouses are currently full.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, you're right! Candidate B is a no-go because of the glasshouses. And Candidate C needs spectroscopy, which I found out is super unreliable right now. Candidate A sounds way safer and more doable!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, your assessment of the hardware is most helpful. Candidate D is equally perilous, as it relies on live colonies—a known catalyst for schedule overruns. Candidate A avoids these biological whims entirely.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Candidate A is the only choice that aligns with historical success. It is a catalogue-style project, which my research shows provides enduring utility, unlike the mechanism-focused goals of B, C, and D.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Servas,,It is rare to see such a clear consensus. Candidate A not only provides a lasting gift to the scientific community but does so using the hands and tools we actually possess.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Midori,,"It really does seem like the best fit. By avoiding the high costs of animal units and the risks of full glasshouses, Candidate A ensures the grant money goes toward the science rather than overhead.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"I'm all in for Candidate A! It doesn't need the broken spectroscopy tools or the crowded glasshouses, so the researchers can actually get to work right away without any annoying delays!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,It is a refreshing change to find a proposal that does not invite disaster. Candidate A's reliance on existing samples and data processing is a masterclass in risk mitigation. I find it quite compelling.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"The evidence is conclusive. Candidates B, C, and D are gambles on mechanisms that are likely to stall. Candidate A is a resource-building project with a proven track record of delivery and long-term utility.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Servas,,"It is a joy to see us in such agreement. When the vision of the project matches the reality of the people and the tools, success becomes a natural flowering. I am content to support Candidate A.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Midori,,"I am also content to support Candidate A. It is the most sustainable choice, ensuring the researcher isn't burdened by the rising costs of animal units or the logistical stress of unavailable facilities.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Kenji,,I'm totally on board! Candidate A is the only one that doesn't run into the facility problems I found. It's the smartest choice to make sure the research actually happens!,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"I concur. Candidate A is the only proposal that doesn't flirt with catastrophe. By bypassing the volatility of live colonies and the fragility of field sites, it ensures the science remains the priority. My vote is for A.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"I concur. Candidate A is the only proposal that prioritizes a tangible, lasting resource over a speculative mechanism. It is the most reliable choice for ensuring the grant delivers a permanent contribution to the field. My vote is for A.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Servas,,Let us then cast our votes with a peaceful heart. I believe Candidate A is the most prudent and promising path for us to take. My vote is for Candidate A.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Midori,,I agree completely. Candidate A is the most realistic and sustainable option given the current financial and facility constraints. I cast my vote for Candidate A.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm voting for Candidate A too! It's the only one that doesn't get stuck behind broken equipment or full rooms. Let's go with the one that can actually get started! My vote is for A!,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"The consensus is absolute, and for once, the logic is as elegant as the science. I have already stated my preference, but for the sake of the record: my vote is for Candidate A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Toshio,,The data is clear. Candidate A is the only proposal that guarantees a lasting contribution while avoiding the pitfalls of the others. I cast my vote for Candidate A.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
